  "reason": "The patient reports persecutory delusions (belief that dispatch and coworkers are tracking and reporting on her) persisting for 2 months, with a prodromal period of about 8 months of subthreshold suspiciousness. Alongside positive symptoms, she demonstrates prominent negative symptoms (avolition, anhedonia, social withdrawal from previously enjoyed activities like choir and softball, psychomotor slowing/sitting motionless) and disorganized, tangential speech with excessive irrelevant detail throughout the interview, consistent with formal thought disorder. There is clear functional decline in both occupational (falling behind on reports, confusing dispatch instructions) and domestic domains (husband assuming all household responsibilities). Mood symptoms (depressive or manic episodes) were explicitly denied, and no substance use, medical illness, or other organic cause was identified, making mood disorder with psychotic features and schizoaffective disorder less likely. The combination of active-phase psychotic symptoms (delusions, disorganized speech) plus negative symptoms, functional impairment, and total duration exceeding 6 months (8-month prodrome + 2-month active phase) meets criteria for schizophrenia. Delusional disorder (F22) is less fitting given the presence of negative symptoms and disorganized speech beyond an isolated non-bizarre delusion.",
